---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { author, authorBio, authorCredentials, authorNotLawyer, authorLocationNote, authorLd } from '../data/author';
---
<Base
  title="About PrenupAnswers — Who Runs It & How It's Funded"
  description="PrenupAnswers is an independent educational site about prenuptial agreements for US couples, written by Francisco Gomes Alves, a pastor and law student in Brazil. Here's who he is and how the site makes money."
  jsonLd={[authorLd]}
>
  <section class="article-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / About</p>
      <h1>About PrenupAnswers</h1>
      <p class="meta">Independent. Educational. Not a law firm — and never will be.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="prose" id="author">
    <h2>Who runs this site</h2>
    <div class="author-profile">
      {
        author.photo ? (
          <img class="author-photo" src={author.photo} alt={author.name} width="140" height="140" />
        ) : (
          <div class="author-photo author-photo--placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
            {author.initials}
          </div>
        )
      }
      <div>
        <h3 class="author-name-lg">{author.name}</h3>
        <p class="author-role-lg">{author.role} · {author.jobTitle} · {author.location}</p>
        <p>{authorBio}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul>
      {
        authorCredentials.map((c) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{c.label}.</strong> {c.detail}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="notice"><strong>Not legal advice.</strong> {authorNotLawyer}</p>

    <p>{authorLocationNote}</p>

    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>PrenupAnswers exists because the internet failed engaged couples.</strong> Search "do I need a prenup"
      and you get three kinds of results: law firms fishing for clients, wedding blogs gliding past the money
      conversation, and forum threads from 2013. None of them answer the actual question.
    </p>
    <p>
      We're an independent educational site for couples in the United States. We explain what prenups cost, how state
      rules differ, what they can and can't protect, and how to raise the subject with a partner without it turning
      into a fight. In plain English. With real numbers. Every guide carries the date it was last reviewed.
    </p>
    <p>
      A note on credentials, because it matters: on the questions couples actually get stuck on — how to bring this
      up without a fight, whether it means you don't trust your partner — the useful experience is pastoral, not
      legal. A lawyer can tell you what is enforceable. Only someone who has sat with couples can tell you what
      actually happens in the conversation. This site is built on that distinction, and says plainly which is which.
    </p>

    <h2>How we're funded</h2>
    <p>
      Two ways, and that's it: affiliate partnerships and display advertising. If you click a link to an online prenup
      service and buy something, we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. See the full
      <a href="/disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Commission never decides what we recommend.</strong> If a cheaper path is the right path for your
      situation, that's what the article says — even when it earns us nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And neither does faith.</strong> The author is a pastor, but this site carries no religious
      recommendations and no religious framing of the decision. It is funded and written as a consumer guide. What
      you believe about marriage is your business; what a prenup costs is arithmetic.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will never do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Sell legal services or draft agreements</li>
      <li>Pretend to be lawyers or offer legal advice</li>
      <li>Hide affiliate links behind vague language</li>
      <li>Present an estimate as a sourced figure — ranges are labelled as estimates</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When you actually need a lawyer</h2>
    <p>
      Online services are fine for straightforward situations. But if one partner owns a business, there's a
      family-trust, complex stock comp, real estate across states, or a prior marriage with children involved — talk
      to a licensed family-law attorney in your state. A few hundred dollars of advice up front is cheap insurance
      for a document that governs your finances for life.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions, corrections, or a source we missed? Email <strong>hello@prenupanswers.com</strong>. Corrections to
      something factually wrong get fixed — if you report one and it is still wrong in a few weeks, email again.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
